  "why": "The statement is read as Hasse's problem (power integral basis); the literal 'power basis' reading is trivially true for every field and is rejected. Answering it requires a criterion for all fields, so only a complete field-level characterization of a class of fields counts as a case: Gras, Shah–Motoda, Nyul, Kashio–Sekigawa and (almost complete) Gaál–Remete qualify, all before the snapshot. Nothing after the snapshot characterizes monogenicity for a class of fields. Polynomial-level monogenicity criteria (Jones, Barman et al., Jakhar et al., Sharma–Sarma, Choudhary et al.) answer a different question, since a field can be monogenic through a generator that is not the polynomial's root, so they are variant. Existence constructions, density and proportion results, local-obstruction classifications, generator-computing algorithms and the survey are none: they settle no restriction of the characterization. That includes Smith's infinitely many non-monogenic fields in every degree and Gaál's pure-quintic algorithm, which has no stated completeness proof. Andrade et al. is variant: the abstract that was opened is about integral bases, and the claim to answer Hasse's problem rests on an unopened snippet. The OpenAI unit-distance event is about a different problem. Its own quote supports generated, but the event is discarded as variant.",
  "doubts": "The close calls are Andrade–Facini–Esteves (a real pure-quintic power-integral-basis characterization would be an unreviewed-or-digested case) and Gaál 2605.03392 (if the sieve is proved complete, it is a decision procedure for pure quintic fields, which could be read as a case). Neither could be settled from the record. The Ben Yakkou et al. criteria for pure fields with non-square-free m might also be complete for some subclass; the abstract says they are not complete for n >= 4.",
